oject aims to excavate okay so the  Maghreb is a fascinating space for many  reasons but what really attracted me to  it is the polyphony and plurality of  cultures religions and languages since  within the Maghreb you find all three  monotheistic religions the Jews first of  all since very early on in fact ever  since before the fall of the Second  Temple so a very old old standing  presence then Islam starting in 649 AD  and Christianity as well both in the  days of the Roman Empire and in the  later days of European colonialism okay  so these three religions are present as  well as the number of languages and  dialects which makes it a particularly  rich cultural area and so my my premise  was really to try and look at this  diversity in the frame of the  Mediterranean and to harness this local  plurality to the broader narrative of  historical contact that existed not just  in the Maghreb but throughout the  Mediterranean starting starting from  ancient times okay and why don't you  feel it was important to write the book  well I felt that my argument allowed me  to address a number of issues that I  think are very relevant and pressing in  contemporary times the first one is this  issue of the Mediterranean in itself  what does it mean exactly  historically in this course is a Western  modernity the Mediterranean has been has  been pictured in different ways but  mostly the image that would be prevalent  was that of a fractured space between a  northern shore that would be European  and belong to that logic and then a  southern shore Islamic Arab Muslim that  would be the other Jews Europe and so my  idea by focusing on the on this region  this transnational space of the  Mediterranean was really to try and  build some kind of alternative discourse  and Reid admitted  Iranian differently alongside this  fractured vision of it so I thought that  it allowed me to really both examine and  critique this notion of the  Mediterranean the two notions of the  Mediterranean that we hear about very  often the first one being that of a  space as I said a fracture and that  would be prevalent in a clash of  civilization type of paradigm where you  look at Europe and the Arab world as  being two incommensurable spaces in  civilizations and then the other side of  the Mediterranean which I'm really  interested in which is the Mediterranean  as a space of mediation and so a space  that would that would of course obey  fractured and a symmetrical logic but  also a logic of cooperation and  collaboration between the two Shores so  I was interested I thought it was  important in fact to investigate both  readings of the Mediterranean and then  on the Maghrebi side of things  I I was interested in trying to find  alternative readings of culture and  identity that would not necessarily  restrict the Maghreb to either a sort of  Penn Arab history with Islam the Arabic  language and a kind of formatted  nationalist sense of identity on the one  hand and on the other hand the relation  between the Maghreb and France I wanted  to see what existed beyond this been  Arab or Franco Maghreb you logic and so  this kind of plural disjunctive reading  of the Maghreb as a Mediterranean space  allowed me to add another dimension to  this discourse how did you do your  methodology well I think that I was very  careful in fact not to impose any kind  of preconceived notion of this trope of  the Mediterranean to really take it  differently at different stages in the  book different moments so the book is  succession of chapters of course that  each focus on one specific moment that  are very specific and that have very  marked idiosyncrasies and so it starts  in the 30s with the school of Algiers  which was a school of writing that  emerged in the Algerian capital around  a number of figures very famous ones  like a deaf community Manuel Robles  Gabriela diesel and then I traced this  throughout the nationalist period in the  50s with deep and Khattab and then in  the 70s and 80s the moments of  disillusion and then in the contemporary  period mm-hm and so I tried for each one  of those chapters to really focus on the  specificities of this moment and to try  to let the text speak to me in a way and  to see where I was getting just looking  at the literature at the cultural  context and so the methodology in this  regard was not necessarily unified but  tried to take each context separately so  what it meant in terms of research was  of course a lot of time in the archive  because I had to go and explore  especially the earlier moments the 30s  the 40s not everything is accessible  easily and so I had to travel I had to  travel to Europe quite a bit to France  to Spain because my original project  also incorporated a little bit of  Spanish in it and so I had to go and  look at periodicals from the from the  time period of different journals and to  see what those authors were writing in  these other media which were very  popular in those days okay and how does  your research differ from other work  that has been done during this time  period I know you touched it a little  bit earlier but can you can you  elaborate on that yes yes a number of  people are talking about the  Mediterranean this days it's one of  those catch-all categories that  everybody seems to be banding about so I  think that it was very important for me  methodologically actually to elaborate  on the previous question to to look at  things to look at the Mediterranean not  just as a geographical space or a theme  so not just text that would be about the  Mediterranean but to look at the  Mediterranean as a heuristic tool or a  specific aesthetic so as a trope  something that would have philosophical  political aesthetic implications and to  try to have this kind of complex and  layered understanding of it so that the  Mediterranean wouldn't just become  another name for diversity or for Franco  Maghrebi crossings which  what a lots of these works end up being  about just another way of looking at how  France and the Maghreb intersect and I  wanted to go beyond I wanted to take  this logic to a trance now onto a  transnational plane and to try to  diffract the paradigm a little more so  that's one first difference the other  difference that I would point out is  that I try to do things styie  chronically starting as I sat with the  30s and tracing it back all the way to  the present and a number of very  contemporary concerns such as  clandestinity the clandestine illicit  migration from Africa and North Africa  to Europe these issues of no more ISM  that have been that have been really  adapted to the contemporary context of  illegal migration and those tragedies  that we read about in the in the  newspapers every day so in this sense I  tried to keep some kind of historical  continuity in it and the last main  difference I would say is that so this  is one book but this is part of a bigger  project and I always try to have an  approach that emphasizes not just French  and Arabic which are the main two  languages of the Maghreb but also other  languages or other idioms and so this  project is technically only about  francophone literature literature  written in French but the last chapter  for instance also includes other  languages and in the and the rest of the  chapters throughout I really try to look  at the way that French intersects with  other cultures other other norms of  their other traditions other idioms as I  said and so it's not just about one  language it's really a plurilingualism  that I am adopting to look at this  corpus okay and what have what have you  found what are some of your conclusions  well one of the things that actually  struck me when I did the research was  the extent to which this trope was  absolutely central to the corpus and  it's son not something that I  anticipated really I knew that it was  very crucial in colonial days because it  was a very easy way to deflect the issue  of  nationalism and whether or not algeria  since most of these people were in  algeria should be independent and so for  cami for instance the mediterranean was  a very very convenient conceptual way  out of this bind since he chose refused  the possibility of independence and  colonialism was becoming untenable so in  the thirties it was understood that i  would find this almost everywhere that i  looked but what I wasn't expecting was  to find it so clearly marked at  different times throughout the history  of the region cultural history of the  region for instance when well in the  contemporary period obviously we've  talked about this across the fields I  think this this obsession almost with  the time for instance of Al Andalus you  know the seventh century rule Arab and  Berber rule over parts of Europe which  year has been really focused on the  Andalusian space as a space of living  together of coexistence can be lengthy  and Spanish and this idea that somehow  it is part of the Maghrebi history and  so in the 90s especially in Algeria  there is this tendency for authors who  do not accept the state of things the  black decade what what has been called a  black decade which is an effective civil  war between Islamists on the one hand  and the power in place the probe rock on  the other and who saw in this Andalusian  utopia a sort of space where another  understanding of identity and culture  could be articulated and so it's it  remained very relevant and very current  all the way to today in fact so that's  one of the things I was really surprised  about another conclusion is that the  Mediterranean is really it evolves very  much it's it's not one single one single  trope it is something that has gone  through history and that can be either  utopian or dystopian for instance that  this is another thing that I wasn't  necessarily necessarily expecting to  find especially in light of what it was  in the 30s and the 40s but this  especially in the recent moment  clandestinity and clandestine crossing  it has become the space of dystopian so  I thought it was important to balance  both representation okay and I know this  is your book is part of a larger  research project that you're working on  tell us a bit about that yes yes well  the project I think all of my projects  there are several really have this  thread in common which is again the  Maghreb and the Mediterranean and the  interface between the two what the other  projects do that this one doesn't do too  much is look at other languages other  corpora and Spanish Italian a number of  dialects event of southern Europe on the  one hand Arabic French of course and  then try to have this kind of pleura  cultural and proof Lurie lingual  approach to things one of the questions  that I think I'm getting more and more  interested in is this idea of diaspora  and this idea of mobility and so getting  out of the Maghreb per se when you look  at what happens when people cross the  sea into Europe to go to France but also  to go to other territories that are not  necessarily historically linked to the  Maghreb and thinking of the Netherlands  for the connection there to America the  United States and I'm interested in  seeing what those non-standard tragic  Tories mean and how they could force us  to rethink our critical practices and so  at the intersection of this plural  plural angle lens and then this interest  in non-standard diasporic patterns I  actually started getting interested in  the production of a certain certain  community of North African authors in  Europe who have decided to write in  other languages than French and Arabic  yes and so they write an Italian in  Catalan in Spanish that's just for the  Mediterranean zone which I am interested  in but if you take it further you find  production in German in Dodge in English  so that is something that is really  under studied at the moment that I think  has a lot of potential and so in fact I  will be holding a conference here at  Yale on this very topic that I think  might be of interest to reviewers yeah  which is going to be cold beyond French  new languages for African diasporic  literature that I'm organizing in  partnership with Mike  professor Christopher Miller was a  professor of French and African and  african-american studies here at Yale  and that will examine this corpus so  corpus written by African authors who  have moved to Europe but not necessarily  to France and who have chosen chosen  other languages of expression than  French or their local native languages  so it will take place in March the last  weekend in March 29th and 30th of March  in the spring it is sponsored by two  councils at the Macmillan Center the  African Studies Council and the Center  on Middle Eastern Council Surrealists  and studies as well as the Kampf on and  a number of national literature  departments and it will gather together  in Everest colors from all three  continents the US Africa and Europe for  two days of panels film screenings  discussions and finally a beautiful  round table with three authors Laila  Laila me who is the Moroccan author who  writes in English who has gained a lot  of Fame here and whose collection of  short stories in particular hope and  other dangerous pursuits is now one of  the men's days of any almost any  syllabus and postcolonial Anglophone  literature in this country she'll be  here talking about her choice to write  in English to leave Morocco yes then pop  Kumar who is a Senegalese after writing  in Italian and who has become one of the  most prominent voices of this new  Italian African Italian community back  in Italy and rashida Elam rabbit who is  a Moroccan as well but a Berber this  time who brides in Dutch from Belgium  Wow and who has two novels who have  garnered award as well and who are being  very very important this new literary  landscape very exciting will have the  Fleur to that yes well thank you very  much for being here and sharing some of  your work Thank You Marilyn for having  me was a pleasure okay for more  information about Professor talam a  Talib I EV and her research please visit  our website at Yale gdu slash Macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Centre for  International and Erised  yeah

